== Subinterpreters vs Multiprocessing
pygrametl already supports parallel ETL processing through multiprocessing @pygrametl-docs. With the introduction of subinterpreters in Python 3.14 @pep734, we investigated whether they could offer performance advantages over multiprocessing for ETL workloads.

We developed seven standalone microbenchmarks to compare multiprocessing and subinterpreters in terms of communication overhead, task distribution, shared identifier generation, CPU-bound scalability, memory consumption, and worker initialisation overhead. Each benchmark was repeated multiple times, with median execution times reported. Where applicable, workers were reused to exclude initialisation overhead.

The results showed that both approaches achieved similar CPU-bound performance, with multiprocessing reaching a speed-up of 4.53 and subinterpreters 4.63 when using eight workers. Subinterpreters also performed better when distributing independent tasks, completing the fan-out benchmark approximately 27% faster with eight workers. Additionally, subinterpreters generally demonstrated lower memory consumption, using 89 MB compared with 129 MB for multiprocessing in the eight-worker CPU benchmark.

However, multiprocessing performed considerably better when transferring dictionaries, which is particularly relevant as pygrametl represents ETL rows using this data structure. For dictionaries containing ten columns, multiprocessing queues achieved approximately 267,000 rows per second, compared with 132,000 for subinterpreter queues. As shown in @fig:dict-transfer, this performance difference was consistent across all tested dictionary sizes. Performance was otherwise largely comparable, with subinterpreters performing better in task distribution. Finally, creating eight workers took approximately 1.61 ms using process forking, compared with 31.46 ms for subinterpreters.

Based on these results, we decided not to pursue a subinterpreter-based implementation for pygrametl. As this would require a substantial rewrite of pygrametl's existing multiprocessing implementation, the experiments did not demonstrate sufficient performance benefits to justify the effort. We therefore chose to investigate other approaches to improving parallel ETL performance.
